mode: "100644"
text: "# The TALK framework — make an avatar that doesn't feel like one\n\nAvatars are the right tool for **scaled, scripted, multilingual delivery** — explainers, training,\nlocalization, FAQ, faceless channels, personalized sales at volume. They're the **wrong** tool for\nspontaneity, personality-led content, and parasocial connection (that's still human territory). The\nTALK framework keeps avatar video useful instead of uncanny — with consent and disclosure as the\nnon-negotiable spine running through all four.\n\n## T — Tailor the avatar to the job\n- **Personal brand / founder voice** → a custom **Digital Twin** (Avatar V from ~15s of footage;\n  holds identity across long videos). **Consent verification required** — only your own or a\n  consented person's likeness.\n- **Generic / faceless** → a **stock avatar** (no likeness questions).\n- **Quick social clip (<15s)** → a **Talking Photo** is fine; beyond that it turns uncanny — use\n  Avatar IV/V.\n- Match the avatar's look/demeanor to **brand-profile** and the audience.\n\n## A — Audio that fits\n- Pick the **voice and language** deliberately; clone the speaker's voice for consistency, or pick a\n  stock voice that matches **voice-builder**.\n- For **localization**, use Video Translator (175+ languages, lip-sync); voice cloning keeps the\n  speaker's voice across languages. (Standalone narration → ai-voiceover.)\n\n## L — Lean script (write for avatar delivery)\n- Avatars deliver **calm, scripted clarity** well — and hype/improv poorly. Write conversational,\n  one idea, short sentences, no tongue-twisters or rapid-fire energy.\n- Open with the point (no \"hey guys\"); keep segments tight. A good avatar script reads like a\n  confident person explaining one thing clearly.\n\n## K — Keep eyes moving\n- A static talking head **fatigues** viewers fast. Cut away: B-roll (via veo-3 / ai-video),\n  screen-recordings, on-screen text, shot/background changes, captions (most watch on mute).\n- Treat the avatar as the **spine** of the video, not the whole frame. Break long scripts into\n  tighter segments.\n\n## The spine: consent + disclosure (never optional)\n- **Only consented avatars** — your own twin, a consented person, stock, or licensed talent. Never a\n  real non-consenting person (deepfake). HeyGen requires consent verification; enforce it yourself.\n- **Always disclose** the avatar is AI (EU AI Act transparency; TikTok auto-disclosure; YouTube\n  Altered-Content). Disclose in caption and/or on-screen.\n\n## Don't lean on one twin\nAI-avatar feeds crowd fast; a single twin sees **CPMs creep and CTRs flatten within weeks.**\nDiversify — multiple avatars, mixed avatar/live-action, statics-and-motion. HeyGen is one input.\n"
